WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:08.478 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Europe capitals, episode two of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:16.384 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Eight capitals run from the Baltic states in the north to Moscow and Chișinău
in the east.

3
00:00:17.047 --> 00:00:18.916 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tallinn into view.

4
00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:30.586 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tallinn stands on the Gulf of Finland in the north of Estonia, across the
water from Helsinki.

5
00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:42.206 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Probably from taani linn, "Danish town", after the Danish conquest of 1219,
its medieval old town is a UNESCO site.

6
00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:50.874 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Tallinn as the "Danish town" on the Gulf of Finland, whose old town
is listed by UNESCO and where Skype was built.

7
00:00:51.543 --> 00:00:53.668 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Riga into view.

8
00:00:57.472 --> 00:01:04.032 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Riga stands on the Daugava river by the Baltic Sea, in the middle of Latvia.

9
00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:12.568 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Founded in 1201 by Bishop Albert of Buxhoeveden, it was one of the great towns
of the Hanseatic League.

10
00:01:13.222 --> 00:01:21.791 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Riga as a Hanseatic trading town, and the place where, by local
tradition, the first decorated Christmas tree stood in 1510.

11
00:01:22.474 --> 00:01:24.726 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Vilnius into view.

12
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:34.828 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Vilnius stands on the Neris river in the south-east of Lithuania, inland from
the Baltic coast.

13
00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:42.734 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for the Vilnia river, its baroque old town is one of the largest in
Europe and a UNESCO site.

14
00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:53.204 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Vilnius for its baroque old town and for Užupis, the district that
declared itself a republic in 1997 with its own constitution.

15
00:01:53.889 --> 00:01:55.549 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Minsk into view.

16
00:01:59.815 --> 00:02:06.421 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Minsk lies on the Svislach river in the centre of Belarus, inland from the
Baltic Sea.

17
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:16.542 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
First mentioned in 1067 and almost destroyed in the Second World War, it was
rebuilt in the grand Soviet style of the 1950s.

18
00:02:17.198 --> 00:02:25.081 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Minsk as a city rebuilt after the war, and the headquarters of the
Commonwealth of Independent States.

19
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.767 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Warsaw into view.

20
00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:42.161 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Warsaw, called Warszawa in Polish, stands on the Vistula river in the middle
of Poland, east of the country's centre of population.

21
00:02:42.817 --> 00:02:49.295 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From a personal name, Warsz. The mermaid legend of Wars and Sawa is a later
folk invention.

22
00:02:49.945 --> 00:03:00.522 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Warsaw as the city rebuilt from paintings and photographs after 85%
of it was destroyed, and the rebuilding itself listed by UNESCO.

23
00:03:01.206 --> 00:03:03.296 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kyiv into view.

24
00:03:07.372 --> 00:03:13.641 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kyiv stands on the Dnipro river in north-central Ukraine, where the forest and
steppe zones meet.

25
00:03:14.295 --> 00:03:26.915 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for Kyi, the eldest of three brothers who legend says founded the city
with their sister Lybid. The Kyivan Rus state here was the origin of both
Ukraine and Russia.

26
00:03:27.577 --> 00:03:37.004 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Kyiv as the city of the three brothers, and the home of the Arsenalna
metro station, one of the deepest in the world at 105 metres.

27
00:03:37.688 --> 00:03:39.557 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Moscow into view.

28
00:03:43.795 --> 00:03:51.895 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Moscow sits inland in western Russia, on the Moskva river, in the middle of
the European part of the country.

29
00:03:52.553 --> 00:04:04.151 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Named for the river, whose own name is probably Finno-Ugric or Baltic, perhaps
meaning wet or marshy, it has been the capital since 1480 except for the two
centuries when St Petersburg was.

30
00:04:04.803 --> 00:04:13.847 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Moscow as the capital that gave St Petersburg two hundred years of
leave, and whose metro stations were built as palaces for the people.

31
00:04:14.532 --> 00:04:17.806 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Chișinău into view.

32
00:04:20.395 --> 00:04:26.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Chișinău stands on the Bîc river in the centre of Moldova, inland from the
Black Sea.

33
00:04:27.618 --> 00:04:41.306 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name is often read as "new spring", from old Romanian words, though that
is uncertain. Most of the city was rebuilt after an earthquake in 1940 and the
Second World War.

34
00:04:41.972 --> 00:04:52.851 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Chișinău as the city whose name may mean new spring, and where
Pushkin lived in exile from 1820 to 1823.

35
00:04:57.638 --> 00:05:08.807 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tallinn, Riga and Vilnius are the three Baltic states. Minsk, Warsaw and Kyiv
lie further south and east, and Moscow and Chișinău close the group.

36
00:05:09.468 --> 00:05:25.272 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the Danish town of Tallinn, the Hanseatic Riga, the Vilnia and the
republic of Užupis, the rebuilt Minsk, the mermaid legend of Warsaw, the three
brothers of Kyiv, and the Moscow that swapped with St Petersburg.
